// A NESTED reserved field (`__drupe.sessionid`, declared by the custom
// event schema) bound by a LITERAL in the temporal predicate — with NO
// `context.` correlation and NO schema pin. This isolates a serialization gap
// adjacent to case 1122: there, a schema pin adds a `context.__drupe.*`
// reference to the same field, which incidentally causes the field to be
// serialized; here there is no such reference, so the field is only ever
// mentioned as a literal-matched predicate arg.
//
// The interpreter matches the arg by descending the event's nested
// `__drupe.sessionid` record directly (`field_path`), so the literal
// "sess-1" matches the past Login and the `formerly` fires.
//
// A differential temporal engine must serialize the nested reserved field into the
// event's `ctx` REGARDLESS of how the policy binds it (literal, var, or
// `context.` correlation) — the compiled predicate reads that `ctx` key in all
// cases. A harness that only serializes reserved paths bound via a
// `context.<...>` correlation leaves the key absent for a literal binding, so
// the predicate's `= 'sess-1'` filter sees NULL and never matches → the leaf
// wrongly denies. This case exists to catch exactly that.
//
// Controls:
//   trace_1: past Login with sessionid "sess-1" (matches the literal) → the
//            current Read is PERMITTED.
//   trace_2: past Login with sessionid "sess-OTHER" (does not match the
//            literal) → NOT permitted (isolates that the literal actually
//            constrains the match, so a spurious permit is not a false pass).

permit (
    principal,
    action == Drupe::Action::"Read",
    resource
)
when temporal {
    formerly within 1h Drupe::Action::"Login"::request{ input.user: context.input.user, __drupe.sessionid: "sess-1" }
};
